  "live_records": {
    "q-96284": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96284",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $U$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-10,10],$ compute $P(U<-3).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $U$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le u\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $U$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-10,10],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20},\\, & -10\\le u\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U<-3) & =P(-10\\le U<-3) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-10}^{-3}f(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-10}^{-3}\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-(-10)}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
