    "q-96333": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96333",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ compute $P(0\\le X\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\pi },\\, & -\\pi \\le x\\le \\pi , \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-0)}{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
