    "q-90631": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90631",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-6,-1],$ compute $P(Y>-5).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le y\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-6,-1],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5},\\, & -6\\le y\\le -1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>-5) & =P(-5<Y<-1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-(-5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
