      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $W$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[0,4],$ compute $P(W\\ge 1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $W$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le w\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $W$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[0,4],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 0\\le w\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\ge 1) & =P(1\\le W\\le 4) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
